     "title": "Two Failure Modes of Uncritical Adoption",
     "content": "Consider two organizations that adopt Leader-Leader language enthusiastically after reading Marquet's book, and both run into trouble for opposite reasons.\n\nThe first is a regional hospital's nursing unit. Leadership announces that nurses should now use intent-based language and feel empowered to make more autonomous calls on patient care escalation, modeled explicitly on the submarine. But the unit has high turnover, a substantial cohort of recently licensed nurses without deep pattern-recognition built from years of qualification-style mentorship, and no equivalent of the submarine's months-long, peer-verified qualification process to build the competence the model assumes. Pushed-down authority without pushed-down competence does not produce better decisions -- it produces well-intentioned but under-supported judgment calls in a domain where errors are irreversible. The organizational lesson is not that Leader-Leader is wrong, but that Marquet's own sequencing -- competence and clarity have to be built deliberately, over real time, before authority follows -- was skipped in favor of just adopting the language.\n\nThe second is a manufacturing plant with a genuine, hard-won safety culture built on strict, centralized lockout-tagout procedures for machinery. A new plant manager, inspired by the submarine story, relaxes several procedural checkpoints in the name of trusting frontline judgment, treating the existing procedure as the kind of bureaucratic over-specification Marquet criticizes. But lockout-tagout procedures exist because the consequence of a single missed step is irreversible physical harm, and the tight coupling between a skipped step and an injury is exactly the profile where centralized, non-negotiable procedure is appropriate, per the model's own internal logic. Here the plant manager mistook a load-bearing safety hierarchy for the kind of low-stakes bureaucratic layering Marquet actually meant to target, and the retreat from strict procedure was the error, not an application of the book's lesson.",
